1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Every call gets its own record on the stack, pushed on entry and popped on return.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Nine fields, forty-four bytes: three parameters, the return address, two links, the return value, two locals, two temporaries.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
The control link points at the caller; the access link points at the lexically enclosing procedure.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Offsets are measured from the frame pointer, and every field starts at its own offset.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
fact of three pushes four fact frames above main, each with its own copy of n.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
The order of calls is a preorder traversal of the activation tree; that is GATE 2021 Set 1.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The order of returns is a postorder traversal, and the values come back as one, one, two, six.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
A frame is freed only after the call it is waiting on returns, so the stack is a stack.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
The 2016 program: a global a, a local a inside m, and n reading an a it never declares.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Static scoping follows the access link to the global a, which is three, so x becomes six and both prints show six.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
Dynamic scoping follows the control link to the a in m, which is two, so x becomes four and both prints show four.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
One program, two rules, two answers: static gives six and six, dynamic gives four and four.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Call by value copies the argument, so the callee can change only its own copy.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
Call by reference copies the address, so the parameter and the argument are one cell.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
Call by value-result copies in and copies back at return, so it differs from reference when two parameters alias.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Two questions decide every parameter question: which mechanism, and is any argument passed twice.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
One operator per statement: the tree for a plus b times c minus d becomes four statements.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Quadruples give the result a name; triples give the result a position instead.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Intermediate code is machine-independent, which is why the optimiser can be reused for another target.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Four statements, four quadruples, and the same value as the tree for every input the model tries.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
A basic block has exactly one entry, at its first statement, and one exit, at its last.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Three rules find the leaders: the first statement, every jump target, and every statement after a jump.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The GATE 2025 sequence has six leaders, so it has six basic blocks, holding one, one, seven, two, one and five statements.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
The partition is checked: every statement lies in exactly one block, and no block is entered in the middle.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
Fold the constants first: four times five becomes twenty, and that exposes a statement nobody uses.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Propagate constants and copies, then delete every assignment whose variable is not live afterwards.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Strength reduction trades a multiplication by a small constant for an addition: two times i becomes i plus i.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Nine statements become four, and both versions are checked to compute the same value for every input tried.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Value numbering notices that b plus c is computed twice and a plus one is computed twice.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The DAG reuses one node per distinct expression, and a second definition becomes a second label on the same node.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Five statements give a DAG of six nodes: three leaves for b, c and one, and three operator nodes.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
Two statements disappear, and the model checks that the DAG computes the same final value as the original code.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A variable is live at a point when some path from there reaches a use without redefining it first.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
The two equations run backwards: IN is use union the difference of OUT and def, and OUT is the union of the successors' IN.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Starting from empty sets, two rounds reach a fixed point; a third round changes nothing.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
The largest live set holds two variables, so two registers suffice, and the interference graph needs two colours.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Available expressions is the mirror image: it runs forwards and intersects at a join.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Union at the join would say a plus b is available, although one path reassigned a.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Four questions classify any data-flow analysis: direction, meet, boundary, and what is killed.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
One checklist for the whole topic: leaders, DAG nodes, live sets, register count.

